told not to  both recovered from their rebounds  quickly  what everyone does agree on is that  we'll need more studies to really figure  out things like  optimal dosage and and duration of  treatment  researchers in other countries are also  developing drugs to combat the  coronavirus  for example scientists in colombia are  working on a protein-based treatment  that's intended to lessen the impact of  covid19 on the body  [Music]  when covet 19 hit colombia many  researchers at this biotechnology  institute dropped other projects to  focus on it and one promising idea could  lead to a possible therapy for the  disease it involves using blocker  proteins to mimic a large enzyme called  ace2 it's where the coronavirus docks on  to break in  apart once you have that knowledge you  can start cutting out the minimum  necessary parts of the ace2 protein  those are the ones that interact with  the viruses spike protein  that's how i started cutting out these  sections  we've now designed about 200 different  versions of what we're calling mini  ace2s  these mini ace2s interact with the virus  in different ways could some of them  reduce its ability to dock onto  vulnerable cells the colombian  researchers ran models of molecular  dynamics and discovered promising  candidates  yeah i say this as well our comparisons  showed that three of those proteins were  very interesting because one had three  times the interaction strength compared  to natural ace2  the second had 20 times the strength and  the last had 40 times the interaction  strength  so the virus's spike protein binds more  efficiently to the blocker proteins than  it does to human cells could that be  used to create an effective medicine  paulino gomez decided to support the  investigation  back when the whole world was searching  for solutions everyone had their own  ideas about the best ways to fight  against sars ko v2 in our lab we were  searching for small molecules that might  be effective against it but didn't have  much success i found cesar's approach  very interesting it comes at the problem  from a different angle and we decided to  go with it  the blocker proteins are churned out by  genetically engineered yeast  it's a relatively cheap technique to  employ  and researchers at a nearby institute  have used it to explore other proteins  and told me he had some candidates that  we would work on to develop an  alternative treatment for covert 19 i  looked forward to transferring our  experience with other proteins other  diseases to this much more global  disease the work could potentially have  a much greater impact that was very  motivating  the project won a local award receiving  funding that speeded up the research  process it also gave the scientists a  regular income which is far from  guaranteed in colombia  participating in a project as ambitious  as this one which could potentially  contribute to scientific knowledge in  general both on the global stage and in  the current pandemic that has given me a  lot of pride  the blocker protein models that were  digitally designed then produced will  next move into testing in human cells  the researchers could hit some speed  bumps at this critical stage  it isn't easy in colombia to import the  necessary reagents the treatment would  be intended for people who have active  covet infections  it would be given to those who've been  infected so someone who's tested  positive for the virus  they would receive the blocker protein  to inhibit the viruses that are  circulating in their bodies or that have  multiplied in affected  blocker would prevent those viruses from  continuing to infect more cells  the goal is to create an effective  treatment for combating the corona virus  but the research is also providing  fundamental insights into a possible new  approach to fighting other diseases  [Music]  we hope this episode could bring you up  to date on treatments against covet 19  and the pandemic's ongoing impact  hop